Bad query that scans everything:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'shipped' AND created_at >= '2024-01-01';
EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows 2.5M rows scanned, 1.2s.

Good rewrite that scans only what matters:
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE created_at >= '2024-01-01' AND status = 'shipped';
Same result. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows 250K rows scanned, 0.15s.

CREATE TABLE users (id serial primary key, org_id int, salary int);
INSERT INTO users SELECT g, (g % 1000), (g % 50000) FROM generate_series(1,1000000) g;
ANALYZE users;

Slow shape: self-join with correlated filter.
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT u1.org_id, u1.salary FROM users u1 JOIN users u2 ON u1.org_id = u2.org_id WHERE u1.salary > 10000 AND u2.salary > 40000;
Result: 2.8s, 12.4M rows examined.

Fast shape: subquery with EXISTS.
EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT DISTINCT u1.org_id, u1.salary FROM users u1 WHERE u1.salary > 10000 AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM users u2 WHERE u2.org_id = u1.org_id AND u2.salary > 40000);
Result: 1.1s, 3.2M rows examined.
